WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.951
Logs are central to Linux forensics, but their location and completeness vary.

00:00:05.951 --> 00:00:12.785
Traditional syslog-style files may appear under /var/log, with files such as syslog, messages, auth.

00:00:12.785 --> 00:00:14.768
log, secure, kern.

00:00:14.768 --> 00:00:16.311
log, daemon.

00:00:16.311 --> 00:00:20.499
log, and service-specific logs depending on the distribution.

00:00:20.499 --> 00:00:29.978
Authentication logs are often critical because they can show SSH activity, sudo use, failed logins, successful sessions, and account-related events.

00:00:29.978 --> 00:00:35.929
Systems that use systemd may also store events in the systemd journal.

00:00:35.929 --> 00:00:46.289
The journal can be reviewed with journalctl, and it may contain service starts, authentication messages, kernel messages, application output, and boot history.

00:00:46.289 --> 00:00:53.563
Analysts should understand whether the journal is persistent or volatile on the system being examined.

00:00:53.563 --> 00:00:59.955
If journal data is not persisted across reboots, important history may be unavailable.

00:00:59.955 --> 00:01:05.466
Application logs can be just as important as operating system logs.

00:01:05.466 --> 00:01:11.858
Web servers may record requests, response codes, user agents, source addresses, and errors.

00:01:11.858 --> 00:01:20.014
Databases, containers, VPN services, identity agents, management tools, and custom applications may all keep their own records.

00:01:20.014 --> 00:01:23.761
Cron logs may reveal scheduled job activity.

00:01:23.761 --> 00:01:28.831
Package manager logs may show software installation, updates, or removal.

00:01:28.831 --> 00:01:36.986
Log rotation matters because older logs may be compressed, renamed, archived, or deleted according to retention settings.

00:01:36.986 --> 00:01:42.938
Missing logs do not automatically prove tampering, but they are worth explaining.

00:01:42.938 --> 00:01:49.771
Analysts should note gaps, unexpected truncation, permission changes, unusual timestamps, and inconsistencies between sources.

00:01:49.771 --> 00:01:58.367
The strongest log analysis compares multiple sources instead of relying on one perfect record that may not exist.
